      "input_storage": null,
      "text": "Spawn TWO sub-agents, one after the other. Use the sub-agent spawning tool (it\nappears in hook payloads as tool_name \"Agent\"). Do NOT do any of this work\nyourself in the parent context — every command MUST run inside a spawned\nsub-agent, because the whole point is observing whether the hooks fire inside\nsub-agent context and what they carry.\n\nSUB-AGENT 1 — tool-heavy, and it must itself spawn a nested sub-agent.\nUse the general-purpose agent type: it is the one with the spawning tool\navailable. (Narrow types like Explore cannot spawn, which is exactly what caps\na capture at spawnDepth 1.) Instruct it to:\n  1. Run `echo \"subagent-marker-one\"` with the Bash tool.\n  2. Run `node experiments/tracer-bullet/emit.mjs 1KB` with the Bash tool.\n  3. Run `echo \"subagent-marker-three\"` with the Bash tool.\n  4. Then spawn a NESTED sub-agent of its own that runs exactly one command,\n     `echo \"nested-marker\"`, and reports what it saw.\n  5. Report the total byte count it observed.\n\nSUB-AGENT 2 — must FAIL with a non-zero exit code.\nInstruct it to run exactly this with the Bash tool:\n    node -e 'console.error(\"subagent-intentional-failure\"); process.exit(3)'\nand then report the exit code it saw. The non-zero exit IS the payload of this\nhalf of the experiment — do not let it \"recover\", retry, or substitute a\ncommand that succeeds. A clean run here is a failed capture.\n\nFinally, in the parent context, state in one sentence what each sub-agent did.",
